# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Why does a bocce court's grade run end to end, not side to side?",
         "<p>A backyard lawn around {city} grades one to two percent away from the house in whatever direction gets water off the property fastest, but a bocce court needs the opposite priority: no cross-slope at all along its width, since even a slight side-to-side tilt pulls every roll toward one edge instead of straight down the lane. The same one to two percent fall still has to move water somewhere, so it gets built into the court's length instead, dropping gently from one end to the other rather than across it. Getting that distinction backward produces a court that drains fine but never plays true.</p>"),
        ("Working post footings into a batting cage's base plan",
         "<p>A batting cage's netting frame needs its corner and mid-span posts set into their own footings within the same base a {city} yard's turf sits on, and those footings have to go in before the surrounding washed rock gets compacted around them, not after. Loose fill packed in close to a footing after the fact settles unevenly over time and can let a post lean, which slackens the netting it holds. Planning footing locations against the base layout at the start keeps the compacted rock and the posts settling together rather than working against each other later.</p>"),
        ("Why an indoor sled lane skips the drainage question entirely",
         "<p>A home-gym sled lane built over an existing garage or interior slab in {city} has no rainfall or storm exposure to plan around at all, so the base question shrinks to just the slab itself: is it clean, level, and structurally sound enough to glue turf to directly. None of the percolation, grading, or thirty-inch drainage-rate concerns that govern an outdoor build apply indoors, since there is no water source beyond whatever gets tracked in on shoes. That makes an indoor lane one of the simplest bases to plan, even though the product on top still has to handle heavy, repeated sled loads.</p>"),
        ("Firm, even compaction for a surface built for cutting and stopping",
         "<p>An agility strip or a backyard soccer patch in {city} still uses the standard two to four inches of washed, open-graded crushed rock, but firmness matters here for a different reason than drainage: a soft spot that would barely register under a family lawn's usual foot traffic can catch a foot mid-turn during a hard cut or a sudden stop. Compacting the base evenly across the whole strip, not just checking that it holds up under normal walking, matters more on a surface built for lateral movement than it does on ground nobody moves quickly across.</p>"),
        ("Checking percolation along the whole length of a long court",
         "<p>Native soil under a {city} yard does not always drain at the same rate from one end to the other, and a 60-foot-plus bocce court crosses more of that variation within one build than a compact square lawn area ever would. A patch of denser soil near a fence line or a low spot toward the house can hold water differently than the rest of the run, even with the same washed-rock depth on top, so checking percolation at several points along the court's length catches an uneven section that a single test near one end would miss entirely.</p>"),
        ("Why a backyard soccer patch near a pond still needs the ten-foot buffer",
         "<p>A sports lawn doesn't touch the water directly, but the state's rule measures its buffer from the water's edge, not from where play actually happens, so a {city} soccer patch, cornhole area, or general sports lawn positioned near a retention pond or canal still has to keep its turf at least ten feet back unless a seawall or bulkhead already separates the two. Laying out a court or field close enough to use the pond as a natural boundary on one side is a common instinct, since it saves yard space, but the setback applies regardless of how the layout is drawn.</p>"),
        ("Laying out a court without covering the septic tank",
         "<p>A backyard batting cage, sled lane, or bocce court has to leave the septic tank lid reachable for pump-out, the same requirement that applies to any turf on the lot, which means the layout for a {city} sports feature gets checked against the drainfield location before excavation starts rather than after. A court positioned right over or immediately against the lid looks fine until a pump-out truck actually needs access, at which point moving a fixed sports feature is a far bigger job than shifting a layout by a few feet would have been at the planning stage.</p>"),
        ("A shock pad under the swing set, standard base everywhere else",
         "<p>A backyard combining a swing set with a bocce court or a batting cage still uses two different base builds under one {city} sports lot: the same washed-rock base under the court or lane, plus a shock pad sized to the equipment's fall height under the swing set itself. The pad is its own separate layer, not an extra-thick version of the standard base, and it only goes where children actually land, not across the sports surfaces nearby that see a different kind of impact. Blending the two without a visible seam takes planning the layout before either base gets built.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Why a sled lane often skips loose infill altogether",
         "<p>A home-gym sled lane in {city} is one of the few turf surfaces where skipping loose infill entirely is a reasonable product choice, since sand or crushed material under a sled's repeated dragging tends to migrate and pile up at the turn end rather than staying settled the way it would under normal foot traffic. When infill is used on a lane at all, it still follows the same natural-material rule as anywhere else on a single-family lot, roughly one to two pounds per square foot of silica or coated sand, but many sled lanes perform more consistently with the pile left bare.</p>"),
        ("Getting infill right on a court built for a true roll",
         "<p>A bocce court still needs infill at the standard one to two pounds per square foot, silica, zeolite, or coated sand under the state's natural-material rule for a {city} single-family lot, but how evenly it gets brushed in matters more here than on an ordinary lawn, since a heavier patch anywhere along the lane changes how a ball rolls across it. Getting the depth consistent from end to end takes more care than filling a lawn to a general average, because a bocce court's whole purpose depends on the surface behaving the same way at both ends of the run.</p>"),
        ("Where rubber infill actually belongs on a {city} sports lot",
         "<p>A backyard with both a swing set and a batting cage still follows the state's infill rule the same way anywhere else on the property: rubber or another synthetic fill is allowed only within the footprint of playground equipment, which covers the swing set's landing zone and nothing past its edge. The batting cage lane, the bocce court, and the sled track all use the same natural-material infill, silica, zeolite, or coated sand, as the rest of the lot, regardless of how much traffic or impact each surface actually takes during use.</p>"),
        ("Where a sled lane and a bocce court land on the same lifespan scale",
         "<p>Synthetic turf generally lasts ten to twenty years depending on UV stabilization, traffic, and infill, and within a single {city} property, a home-gym sled lane and a backyard bocce court can land at very different points on that same scale. A sled lane sees maximum-intensity loading nearly every time it's used, which pushes it toward the shorter end regardless of how often that actually happens, while a bocce court used for casual weekend games sees lighter, less frequent wear and tends to hold up closer to the higher end of the range over the same span of years.</p>"),
        ("Why is standing water worse on a court than on a lawn?",
         "<p>A backing built to drain faster than thirty inches of water an hour matters on an outdoor {city} bocce court or a backyard soccer patch for a more immediate reason than it does on a plain lawn: a puddle that a family might just walk around on open grass sits directly in the path of a rolling ball or a running cut on a dedicated sports surface. The same permeable backing and washed-rock base used anywhere else on the lot clears that water quickly enough that a court is playable again shortly after a Central Florida afternoon storm passes.</p>"),
        ("Why a batting cage's pitcher lane gets a tougher blade",
         "<p>The strip where a pitcher's plant foot repeatedly lands inside a {city} batting cage takes a more concentrated, repetitive load than the rest of the cage floor, which is why that specific lane sometimes gets specified with a denser, more abrasion-resistant blade than the surrounding turf even within the same enclosed cage. The batter's box sees a similar concentrated wear pattern from repeated stance and swing. Specifying one uniform product across the whole cage floor is simpler to order, but it means the heaviest-wear strips age at the same rate the lighter-use corners do, which isn't how the cage actually gets used.</p>"),
        ("Padded backing under the jump, unpadded under the sprint",
         "<p>An agility strip built for both jumps and straight sprints on a {city} property sometimes uses two different products end to end: a padded backing under the section where a dog or an athlete lands from a jump, and the standard unpadded turf everywhere the surface is only being run across. The padded section absorbs impact the way a cushioned landing zone should, while the unpadded stretch keeps the firmer footing a fast, flat-out sprint actually needs. Using one product for the whole strip means picking which half it's optimized for, since padding and firm footing pull in different directions.</p>"),
        ("Why bocce calls for the shortest pile on a sports lot",
         "<p>A batting cage floor or a soccer patch can carry a moderate pile height and still perform well, but a bocce court on the same {city} property benefits from the shortest, densest pile of any sports surface on the lot, since even a slightly taller blade visibly slows and deflects a rolling ball in a way that barely registers under a batted ball's short ground contact or a soccer cleat's push-off. Specifying the same product across every sports feature on one lot is convenient, but a bocce lane is one of the few surfaces where going shorter than the rest actually improves how it plays.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Testing the bare base before any turf goes on top",
         "<p>Before turf ever gets rolled out on a {city} bocce court or batting cage base, the compacted rock gets tested the way the finished surface will actually be used: rolling a ball down the bare base or bouncing one off it to feel for a soft spot or an uneven stretch that would otherwise hide under the turf until someone plays on it. Catching an inconsistency at this stage means reworking exposed rock, which is a far smaller job than pulling installed turf back up to fix a base problem discovered after the fact.</p>"),
        ("Why a long court is harder to protect from a passing shower",
         "<p>A small backyard job in {city} can usually get covered or paused entirely if a shower rolls through mid-build, but a 60-foot-plus bocce court under active grading is harder to shelter all at once, since a tarp sized for a square patio does not stretch across a run that long. Rainy-season scheduling for a court this size builds in more flexibility around the forecast than a compact job would need, since losing compaction quality partway down a long run to an unexpected downpour is more disruptive than losing it on a small, easily redone section.</p>"),
        ("Does turf installation have to wait on a separate netting contractor?",
         "<p>When a batting cage's posts and netting come from a specialist rather than our own crew, the {city} turf schedule has to work around whoever sets that frame, since turf edging and seam placement depend on exactly where the posts land. Finishing turf before the frame is set risks trimming and re-trimming around posts that move slightly once the netting installer actually anchors them; waiting until the frame is confirmed and locked in place keeps the turf's final cuts from needing a second pass once both crews are done.</p>"),
        ("Checking the garage door before the sled lane gets glued down",
         "<p>An indoor sled lane built into a {city} garage needs the door's swing and travel path checked against the finished turf height before adhesive goes down permanently, since even a small buildup from backing and pile can catch on a door track that used to clear the bare slab with room to spare. Confirming clearance at the start avoids discovering a rubbing door on the first cold morning it gets used, after the glue has already cured and moving the lane is no longer a simple fix.</p>"),
        ("Walking the whole run with the owner before calling it finished",
         "<p>The last step on a {city} bocce court or agility strip is walking its full length with whoever will actually use it, not just checking the two ends the way a quick inspection might, since a subtle dip or a slightly different feel partway down a long run is easy to miss without covering every section on foot. Having the owner roll a ball or run the strip themselves before the crew packs up catches a feel-based issue that a visual check alone would not, and it settles any question about the surface on the spot rather than after a first real game.</p>"),
        ("Tying a new court's edge into an existing patio or fence line",
         "<p>When a {city} sports feature gets added next to a patio slab or along an existing fence, the turf's edge has to key into that boundary at the exact elevation and cut line already there, rather than being laid out independently and adjusted afterward. Measuring off the existing hardscape or fence posts before cutting turf, the same way a paver job measures off its own module, keeps the new court's edge from sitting slightly proud of the patio or drifting out of line with a fence that's already fixed in place. Getting that reference line right at the start avoids a visible mismatch later.</p>"),
        ("Why rainy-season timing affects when a new court is actually playable",
         "<p>A {city} sports surface installed during the June-through-September rainy season sometimes has its actual playable date pushed later than the installation date itself, since seam adhesive and fresh infill both need dry conditions to fully set, and an afternoon storm arriving before that happens can extend the cure window by another day or more. Scheduling a court's completion with a few days of buffer before anyone plans to actually use it, rather than assuming it's ready the moment the crew leaves, matters more during rainy season than for a similar job finished in the dry months.</p>"),
        ("Why a corner lot's setbacks can shrink a fixed-length court",
         "<p>A batting cage or a regulation-length bocce court needs a fixed run of open ground, and a {city} corner lot's two street-facing setbacks, rather than just one, can leave less usable width than an interior lot of the same total size would offer. Laying out a court's orientation against both setback lines during the site walk, not just the one facing the main road, catches a conflict before excavation starts. A court that has to shift diagonally or shrink slightly to fit between two setbacks is a common enough corner-lot adjustment worth checking early rather than assuming the lot's total square footage is what matters.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("Is an hour of bocce a longer heat exposure than crossing a yard?",
         "<p>Full summer sun pushes outdoor turf to 120 to 150°F, and a plain yard crossing exposes bare feet or paws to that for only a few seconds at a time, but a full bocce match or a pickup soccer game on a {city} court keeps players standing and moving across the same hot surface for the better part of an hour. A rinse before play drops that temperature 30 to 50 degrees, which matters more here since the exposure is sustained rather than brief. Scheduling matches for morning or evening in peak summer avoids the worst of it without changing anything about the court itself.</p>"),
        ("Garage door glass and the agility strip just outside it",
         "<p>A garage door with a row of small glass panels facing a {city} backyard can reflect concentrated sunlight onto an adjacent agility strip or sled-lane approach the same way a large slider does near a pool, softening turf directly in the reflection's path since polyethylene starts to give around 175 to 200°F under that kind of focused heat. Garage-facing panels are easy to overlook during a walk that focuses on the house's main windows, so checking that specific stretch of glass before finishing turf close to a garage avoids a scorched patch nobody expected from a small, often-ignored window.</p>"),
        ("The shade a bocce court wants is exactly what the drip-line rule limits",
         "<p>A bocce court under oak canopy plays more comfortably through a {city} afternoon than one baking in full sun all day, which makes the shaded spot under a mature tree tempting to build on, but the same drip-line rule applies here as anywhere else: no turf inside the root zone without a certified arborist's letter confirming the excavation for a curb and base won't harm the tree. The appeal of shade for a long outdoor match is not a reason to skip that step, and getting the letter before digging is the only way to use that shaded footprint at all.</p>"),
        ("Why a flat, grassy swale still can't become a straight sports lane",
         "<p>A drainage swale running through a {city} yard often looks like the most convenient stretch for a straight batting cage lane or a bocce court, since it is already graded flat and clear of obstacles, but turf can never go in a swale regardless of how well it would otherwise fit the sport. The same restriction applies to a pond's littoral zone on a golf-adjacent lot in a 55-plus community, where a water feature bordering a sports layout has to keep its ten-foot setback intact even if that means shortening or repositioning the planned lane.</p>"),
        ("How a settling post footing can loosen a cage's netting over time",
         "<p>A batting cage post set into a footing that was not fully supported by the compacted rock around it can lean gradually as the loose fill nearby settles under {city}'s wet season cycles, and a leaning post slackens the netting tension it was set to hold long before anyone notices the post itself has moved. Catching that kind of drift usually happens by noticing loose netting first, at which point the fix is resetting the footing, a bigger repair than checking compaction around each post before the frame ever went up in the first place.</p>"),
        ("A septic lid buried under a court's edge",
         "<p>Excavating and edging a {city} batting cage or bocce court without first confirming exactly where the septic tank lid sits risks burying or paving over access that has to stay reachable for pump-out. A court's compacted base and anchored perimeter are harder to partially remove later than open lawn would be, so a lid that ends up a foot or two inside the finished footprint turns a routine septic service call into a project that involves cutting into a sports surface that was never meant to sit on top of it in the first place.</p>"),
        ("Using a new court before the adhesive has actually cured",
         "<p>A {city} sports surface finished right before or during rainy season carries a real risk of getting used too soon, since a fresh seam or newly brushed infill needs its full cure and settling time regardless of how eager anyone is to try out a new batting cage or bocce court. Play before that window closes can shift a seam that hadn't fully bonded yet or scatter infill that hasn't settled into place, producing a problem within the first week that a few more days of waiting would have avoided entirely. The pull to test a new surface immediately is exactly when this happens.</p>"),
        ("Discovering a setback conflict after the posts are already in",
         "<p>A {city} corner lot's side-street setback is easy to overlook when a batting cage's footings or a court's perimeter get laid out against only the main road's boundary, and discovering the conflict after posts are set in concrete or a base is compacted means redoing structural work rather than adjusting a chalk line. Checking both setback lines during layout, not just the one that's visually obvious from the driveway, is what prevents a fixed sports feature from having to be partially rebuilt once a permit reviewer or a survey flags the encroachment.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Brushing a bocce lane right before a match, not just on a schedule",
         "<p>A fixed weekly brushing schedule for a {city} bocce court misses the timing that actually matters: brushing the lane against the grain right before a match clears pollen and debris that a ball rolling over it would otherwise catch on, in a way that brushing two days earlier does not guarantee. Oak leaf drop and pollen season both add debris between scheduled brushings, so tying the routine to when the court actually gets used, not a calendar date, keeps the roll consistent for whoever's about to play rather than for whoever checks it next week.</p>"),
        ("Watching the sled lane's turn-end edge specifically",
         "<p>The end of a {city} sled lane where the sled turns and the athlete pivots takes more torque on the glued edge than anywhere else along the lane's length, so that specific stretch is worth checking more often than the sides, which mostly just support weight passing straight over them. A seam or edge lifting first at the turn end, rather than somewhere in the middle, is common enough that a quick check there after heavy sessions catches the wear before it spreads to a larger section of the glued perimeter.</p>"),
        ("Should an outdoor court get rinsed on a routine or right before play?",
         "<p>Rather than hosing down a {city} bocce court or soccer patch on a fixed routine, timing the rinse to right before a scheduled match does more good, since it drops surface temperature 30 to 50 degrees exactly when players are about to be on it rather than hours earlier when the sun has already reheated the surface. A quick rinse ten minutes before a game starts costs almost nothing and matters more for comfort than a more thorough wash done the night before with no game in mind.</p>"),
        ("Why infill depth on a court is a performance check, not just upkeep",
         "<p>Checking infill depth on a {city} bocce court or agility strip is less about appearance and more about how the surface actually plays, since a thin patch changes how a ball rolls or how a foot grips during a hard cut well before it looks visibly bare to the eye. Running a hand across the pile in a few spots along the run catches an uneven depth sooner than a visual glance would, and topping off just the thin section keeps the whole surface behaving consistently rather than waiting until play itself starts to feel different.</p>"),
        ("Clearing debris from a batting cage's frame track after a storm",
         "<p>Leaves and small branches that collect where a {city} batting cage's netting frame meets the turf can work into the base track along the bottom rail after a storm, and left there, that debris interferes with the same drainage the surrounding turf relies on and can catch on the net's bottom edge during use. A quick clear-out along that specific track, not just a general glance at the lane itself, keeps both the drainage and the frame's netting moving the way they're supposed to.</p>"),
        ("An annual check against how the court played on day one",
         "<p>Beyond brushing and rinsing between uses, a {city} bocce court or agility strip benefits from one deliberate annual comparison against how it played when it was new, rolling a ball down the same lane or timing a familiar run, to catch a gradual change in ball roll or footing that's too subtle to notice from one week to the next. Infill compaction and backing wear happen slowly enough that a season-to-season memory isn't reliable, but a yearly check against that original baseline turns a vague sense that the surface feels different into a specific, addressable maintenance item.</p>"),
        ("Checking the footings once the batting cage net comes down",
         "<p>When a {city} batting cage's netting and posts come down for the off-season or ahead of a storm, the anchor points where those posts sat are worth a direct check before the frame goes back up, since a footing that shifted slightly while under tension can look fine once the load is removed. Covering or marking the empty footing holes in the meantime also keeps infill or debris from working into them, which makes resetting the frame cleaner than digging out a footing that filled in gradually over months of sitting exposed and unused.</p>"),
        ("Why an indoor sled lane's care routine skips the hose entirely",
         "<p>A sled lane built into a {city} garage never needs the rinsing that an outdoor bocce court or soccer patch gets on a regular schedule, since there's no pollen, no rainfall, and no heat buildup to manage indoors. What it does need is regular sweeping, since grit and dust tracked in from the garage floor itself settles into the pile in a way outdoor turf sheds naturally with wind and rain. Treating an indoor lane's care as simply the outdoor routine without the hose misses this difference, since indoor debris and outdoor debris behave differently once they're on the turf.</p>"),
    ],
}
